with output to target/release/jolt.build/stdlib/grenadine_coordinate.so\n1196-test (1/4)\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-11-19T13:10:45.0556352Z compiling target/release/jolt.build/stdlib/grenadine_gitlibs.scm with output to target/release/jolt.build/stdlib/grenadine_gitlibs.so\n1197-test (1/4)\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-11-19T13:10:45.0557020Z compiling host/chez/java/enum-colls.ss with output to target/release/jolt.build/stdlib/lazy_host_chez_java_enum-colls_ss.so\n1198-test (1/4)\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-11-19T13:10:45.0557720Z compiling host/chez/java/jutil-concurrent.ss with output to target/release/jolt.build/stdlib/lazy_host_chez_java_jutil-concurrent_ss.so\n1199-test (1/4)\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-11-19T13:10:45.0558394Z compiling host/chez/java/array-deque.ss with output to target/release/jolt.build/stdlib/lazy_host_chez_java_array-deque_ss.so\n1200-test (1/4)\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-11-19T13:10:45.0559025Z compiling host/chez/java/zip-gzip.ss with output to target/release/jolt.build/stdlib/lazy_host_chez_java_zip-gzip_ss.so\n1201-test (1/4)\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-11-19T13:10:45.0559619Z compiling host/chez/java/process.ss with output to target/release/jolt.build/stdlib/lazy_host_chez_java_process_ss.so\n1202-test (1/4)\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-11-19T13:10:45.0560278Z compiling host/chez/java/concurrency-sync.ss with output to target/release/jolt.build/stdlib/lazy_host_chez_java_concurrency-sync_ss.so\n1203-test (1/4)\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-11-19T13:10:45.0560953Z compiling host/chez/java/atomic-arrays.ss with output to target/release/jolt.build/stdlib/lazy_host_chez_java_atomic-arrays_ss.so\n1204-test (1/4)\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-11-19T13:10:45.0561418Z build-jolt: embedding boots + stub, linking\n1205-test (1/4)\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-11-19T13:10:45.0561675Z build-jolt: wrote target/release/jolt\n1206:test (1/4)\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-11-19T13:12:08.7835178Z make[1]: *** [Makefile:1986: gambitunbound] Error 1\n1207:test (1/4)\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-11-19T13:12:08.7835670Z gambit unbound: 468 name(s) reported by the linker, 408 record-generated, 60 unbound\n1208-test (1/4)\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-11-19T13:12:08.7835951Z \n1209:test (1/4)\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-11-19T13:12:08.7836570Z UNBOUND — referenced by the boot, defined nowhere, and not allowlisted:\n1210-test (1/4)\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-11-19T13:12:08.7838163Z   ##gc\n1211-test (1/4)\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-11-19T13:12:08.7838374Z \n1212-test (1/4)\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-11-19T13:12:08.7838699Z Bind each one (a mirror in host/gambit/rt-core.ss, a shim in prelude-shims.ss,\n1213-test (1/4)\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-11-19T13:12:08.7839400Z a named raise in host-vars.ss), or record a genuinely untaken path with\n## Stance on LLM Use in an Jolt\n\nThere is a view that code written by hand must be more intentional, almost by definition, since it requires the maintainer to put it there personally. That is, of course, true, but once a project grows past a certain size or has multiple maintainers, no one really has the totality of the code in their heads. Any new code that is added is therefore always done with limited understanding. Code being written by hand should not be equated with faithfully expressing intent. If that were the case, we would never have software bugs. Humans make mistakes all the time, as is clearly evidenced by the abundance of buggy code that predates LLM use.\n\nIt is simply not feasible for humans to keep the entirety of large projects in their heads all at once. When working on a project, one is constantly forgetting and relearning code along the way. The situation is even worse for projects where multiple people work together and no one knows what everyone else was thinking. We look at the code and try to build up sufficient context in our heads to make the necessary changes. When we misjudge that context or misunderstand existing code, we end up making mistakes.\n\nThe reality is that neither code produced by a human nor code produced by an LLM can be trusted to be free of defects. The way we ensure software quality is through a solid development process. The focus has to be on ensuring the quality of the code through code reviews, specifications, testing, and other well established practices. All of these same tools work just as well with LLM-generated code as they do with code written by hand.\n\nThe core skill in using these tools is the same as the skill needed when writing code manually. One has to understand the given requirement, the shape of the solution, and how to verify that the code produced actually matches the intent.\n\nWhat these tools do is automate the mechanical aspect of producing the code. Once it is written, you can read it, understand it, and change it as you would with any other code. The intention and the ideas come from the human using the tool, and new ways to automate the work of typing out code do not change the role or the responsibility of the developer.\n\nFocusing on how the code was produced rather than on the code itself dilutes the responsibility since blame should never be deflected to LLMs. The ownership of the code rests with the person submitting the code, and they must take responsibility for its quality and correctness. Either the code is clean and lives up to the standards of the project, or it will be rejected and the author will be asked to make changes to address the problems.\n_arm#.scm\n_asm#.scm\n_assert#.scm\n_base64\n_codegen#.scm\n_csv\n_define-library\n_define-syntax.scm\n_digest\n_eval#.scm\n_fifo#.scm\n_gambit#.scm\n_gambit.c\n_gambitgsc.c\n_gambitgsi.c\n_geiser\n_git\n_hamt\n_http\n_io#.scm\n/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/gambit/4.9.8/lib/_gambit#.scm\n/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/gambit/4.9.8/lib/_gambit.c\n/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/gambit/4.9.8/lib/_gambitgsc.c\n/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/gambit/4.9.8/lib/_gambitgsi.c\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
